    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Ferdinand I created the kingdom in 1816 by annulling the Sicilian constitution of 1812 and merging the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a> and the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> into one state ruled from Naples.</li><li>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a>–<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/portici">Portici</a> line of 1839 was the first railway on the Italian peninsula, yet by 1859 the kingdom had only 99 kilometres of track against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piedmont">Piedmont</a>'s 850.</li><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giuseppe-garibaldi">Garibaldi</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/expedition-of-the-thousand">Expedition of the Thousand</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> in 1860 and won the Battle of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volturno">Volturno</a> with half its army local volunteers; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-ii">Francis II</a> surrendered at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaeta">Gaeta</a> in February 1861.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>'s mines near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catania">Catania</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agrigento">Agrigento</a> supplied most of the world's sulphur; when Ferdinand II granted a French company the monopoly in 1840, breaking an 1816 trade agreement with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, the Sulphur Crisis followed and was settled only by French mediation.</p></aside>
    </section>

