    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-i-of-the-two-sicilies"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ferdinand-i-of-the-two-sicilies.jpg" alt="Ferdinand I of the Two Sicilies" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-i-of-the-two-sicilies">Ferdinand I of the Two Sicilies</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mengs_-_Ferdinand_IV_of_Naples,_Royal_Palace_of_Madrid.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Anton Raphael Mengs, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Kingdom of the Two Sicilies was made in 1816, when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-bourbon">Bourbon</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-i-of-the-two-sicilies">Ferdinand IV of Naples</a>, restored to his mainland throne by the Treaty of Casalanza and given back the island of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>, annulled the Sicilian constitution of 1812 that had virtually disempowered him there and merged his two realms into one state, the Regno delle Due Sicilie, taking the title Ferdinand I. The name reached back to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicilian-vespers">War of the Sicilian Vespers</a> (1282–1302), after which the Angevin rulers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a> and the Aragonese rulers of the island each called their realm the Kingdom of Sicily, so the two were known together as Utraque Sicilia, “both Sicilies”.</p><p>The Bourbons had ruled in Naples since Infante Charles defeated the Austrians at Bitonto in 1734, the first king in over 230 years to live and rule there in person; when he became King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> in 1759 he left the crown to his younger son Ferdinand IV, since treaties forbade uniting Spain with Naples-Sicily. Driven out in the Napoleonic years, Ferdinand kept some of the reforms of Kings Joseph and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joachim-murat">Joachim Murat</a> to the civil, penal and commercial codes and extended them to Sicily, but inherited brigandage and the forcible occupation of lands. The new kingdom was the largest sovereign state in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> by population and area, covering Sicily and virtually all the peninsula south of the Papal States, with Naples, Italy's biggest city, as its capital.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/olive-oil"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/olive-oil.jpg" alt="Olive oil" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/olive-oil">Olive oil</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Oliven_V1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Poyraz 72, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The population rose from about five to seven million in the nineteenth century, roughly 36 per cent of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>'s around 1850, though figures before a statistical department was founded after 1848 rest on estimates. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a> had over 400,000 inhabitants, the third largest city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palermo">Palermo</a> was Italy's third. The economy was agricultural and protectionist. Land lay in the hands of a few noble families, and the villages housed a large rural <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/proletariat">proletariat</a> dependent on landlords for work. Wheat, wine, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olive-oil">olive oil</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> were the chief products: 67 million litres of oil in 1844, mostly from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apulia">Apulia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calabria">Calabria</a>, and 191 million litres of wine.</p><p>Industry was scattered but real. The shipyard of Castellammare di Stabia employed 1,800 workers; Pietrarsa, the largest plant on the peninsula, made tools, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannon">cannon</a>, rails and locomotives; the Mongiana ironworks in Calabria, founded in 1770, employed 1,600 in 1860 and rolled the rails of the first Italian railways. Swiss entrepreneurs ran a textile hub around Salerno. Sicilian sulphur met most of the world's demand. With every major city a port, the kingdom owned the largest merchant fleet in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>, yet poor interior roads hampered internal trade, and by 1859 it had only 99 kilometres of railway against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piedmont">Piedmont</a>'s 850. Schooling was not compulsory: 2,010 primary schools served nine million people in 1859, and literacy stood at 14.4 per cent in 1861.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/expedition-of-the-thousand"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/expedition-of-the-thousand.jpg" alt="Expedition of the Thousand" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/expedition-of-the-thousand">Expedition of the Thousand</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Partenza_da_Quarto.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Army of the Two Sicilies descended from the force the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-bourbon">Bourbons</a> settled in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-polish-succession">War of the Polish Succession</a>, and at its peak the kingdom kept 100,000 soldiers and a large bureaucracy. The Real Marina was the most important of the pre-unification Italian navies. The army's record was one of internal repression. It put down the Calabrian revolution of 1847, the Sicilian independence revolution of 1848 and, with hired <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swiss-guards">Swiss Guards</a>, the Neapolitan revolution of May 1848, retaking the mainland by July, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/messina">Messina</a> by September and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palermo">Palermo</a> on 15 May 1849.</p><p>Against the Carbonari rising of 1820–1821 it had been less sure: resistance by regular forces under General Guglielmo Pepe and irregular Carbonari was smashed by the 50,000 Austrian troops who entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a> on 24 March 1821, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-vienna">Congress of Vienna</a> had given <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> the right to station troops in the kingdom, which it did until 1827. The war declared on Austria on 7 April 1848, under the pressure of public sentiment, was fought on paper only. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giuseppe-garibaldi">Giuseppe Garibaldi</a> landed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> in 1860 at the head of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/expedition-of-the-thousand">Expedition of the Thousand</a>, with the connivance of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camillo-benso-count-of-cavour">Cavour</a>, many rallied to him; he crossed to the mainland and won the Battle of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volturno">Volturno</a> with half his army local volunteers, and the Bourbon army collapsed. King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-ii">Francis II</a> withdrew to the fortified port of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaeta">Gaeta</a> and surrendered after the Siege of Gaeta in February 1861.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/secret-society"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/secret-society.jpg" alt="Secret society" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/secret-society">Secret society</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Secret_Society_Buildings_New_Haven.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Miss Alice Donlevy, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Two Sicilies was an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/absolute-monarchy">absolute monarchy</a> under the House of Bourbon-Two Sicilies, a cadet branch of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-bourbon">Bourbons</a>, ruled by Ferdinand I (1816–1825), Francis I (1825–1830), Ferdinand II (1830–1859) and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-ii">Francis II</a> (1859–1861). The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-vienna">Congress of Vienna</a> had granted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> the right to station troops, and Austria, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> insisted that no written constitution be given. Administratively <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> remained separate units, the peninsula in fifteen departments and Sicily in seven; Sicily kept a secondary and semi-colonial status beneath the court at Naples.</p><p>Open political activity was suppressed, so liberals organised in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/secret-society">secret societies</a> such as the Carbonari, outlawed in 1816. Their rising of 1820 forced Ferdinand I to grant the Spanish constitution of 1812 on 13 July, which he cancelled on 23 February 1821 before Austrian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayonet">bayonets</a>; repression then deepened. Ferdinand II began with a political amnesty and tax cuts, but after the revolution of 1848, when he appointed a liberal prime minister and broke with Austria only to turn the Swiss Guard on Naples on 15 May, he governed cautiously and authoritatively to forestall another rising. More than half the deputies elected in 1848 were arrested or fled, and the treatment of political prisoners and “suspicious elements” violated the constitution's guarantees. Clerics inspected the schools and held a veto over the appointment of teachers, who were mostly clergy.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/teatro-di-san-carlo"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/teatro-di-san-carlo.jpg" alt="Teatro di San Carlo" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/teatro-di-san-carlo">Teatro di San Carlo</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Teatro_de_San_Carlos,_N%C3%A1poles,_Italia,_2023-03-25,_DD_184-186_HDR.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Diego Delso, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Two Sicilies left its deepest mark in music and in a record of Italian firsts. The Real <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teatro-di-san-carlo">Teatro di San Carlo</a>, opened on 4 November 1737 as the biggest opera house in the world, was rebuilt within ten months of the fire of 13 February 1816 by Antonio Niccolini as a horseshoe of 1,444 seats. Gioachino Rossini was house composer and artistic director of the royal opera houses from 1815 to 1822 and wrote ten operas there; Domenico Barbaja's offer brought Gaetano Donizetti to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a> until 1844, where 51 of his operas were presented, and Vincenzo Bellini's first professionally staged opera opened at the San Carlo on 30 May 1826.</p><p>The kingdom built the first railway on the Italian peninsula, from Naples to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/portici">Portici</a> in 1839, the first iron suspension bridge in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, the Real Ferdinando Bridge, and the first volcano observatory in the world, the Osservatorio Vesuviano, in 1841. Its sulphur fed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>'s industrialisation after the repeal of the salt tariffs in 1824, and Ferdinand II's grant of a monopoly to a French company in 1840 brought the Sulphur Crisis with Britain. Gladstone's letters from Naples in 1850, calling the government “the negation of God erected to a system of government”, helped isolate the kingdom before its conquest, and the title of King of the Two Sicilies is still claimed by the head of the House of Bourbon-Two Sicilies.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/giuseppe-garibaldi"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/giuseppe-garibaldi.jpg" alt="Giuseppe Garibaldi" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/giuseppe-garibaldi">Giuseppe Garibaldi</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_of_Giuseppe_Garibaldi.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Salvatore Lo Forte, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The kingdom's political movements had been Neapolitan or Sicilian rather than Italian: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> in 1848–1849 sought independence from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a>, not a united <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, and public feeling for unification was low, so the Two Sicilies did not figure in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camillo-benso-count-of-cavour">Cavour</a>'s early plans. What undid it was isolation abroad and repression at home. Ferdinand II kept the political prisoners whose release <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> demanded in 1856, and both powers broke off relations. Only when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> was defeated in 1859 and the north, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venetia">Venetia</a> apart, was united in 1860 did <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giuseppe-garibaldi">Garibaldi</a> launch the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/expedition-of-the-thousand">Expedition of the Thousand</a> against Sicily with Cavour's connivance. Many rallied to him, and after Sicily he won the Battle of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volturno">Volturno</a> on the mainland.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-ii">Francis II</a> withdrew to the fortified port of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaeta">Gaeta</a>, where he surrendered and abdicated in February 1861 after the Siege of Gaeta. At the encounter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teanum-sidicinum">Teano</a> Garibaldi met King Victor Emmanuel and handed him the conquered kingdom; the people voted in a plebiscite to join the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a>, and the annexation completed the first phase of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italian-unification">Italian unification</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-italy">Kingdom of Italy</a> being proclaimed in 1861. The south entered the new state with its land concentrated in few hands, cities with little industry to absorb a countryside growing faster than Naples itself, 99 kilometres of railway and a literacy rate of 14.4 per cent. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-bourbon">Bourbon</a> claim to the throne survives in the House of Bourbon-Two Sicilies.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Kingdom of the Two Sicilies united <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-bourbon">Bourbons</a> from 1816, the largest state in pre-unification <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, home to the San Carlo opera, Italy's first railway and most of the world's sulphur; absolutist and repressive, it crushed the revolutions of 1820 and 1848 but fell to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giuseppe-garibaldi">Garibaldi</a>'s Thousand in 1860 and was annexed to the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a> in 1861.</em></p>

